\f1\b\fs28 Request\f0\b0\fs24 \par
Hi - cure rates keep dropping and we missed target again in June. Agents are still calling the most overdue accounts first. Can you run our propensity-to-pay pipeline on the Jan-Jun collections extract and give me a June work list that fits what the 25 agents can handle (about 37% of the cohort), so they focus where the money is? It has to hold up on future months and the probabilities need to be trustworthy because we use them for expected loss. Brief, data dictionary and the deck template are attached.\par
\par
\f1\b\fs28 Task Description\f0\b0\fs24 \par
Pre-existing assets: collections extract of 20,000 accounts entering collections January-June 2026 (input 1), data dictionary (input 2), collections strategy brief (input 3), summary deck template (input 4), and the team's existing propensity-to-pay pipeline in 2_Code (pipeline_config.py and notebooks 01 preprocessing, 02 training, 03 testing, 04 work list and packaging) built for last year's extract and not yet run on this one. The original code is delivered as input 5 and the corrected code as final output 4.\par
1. Review the strategy brief (Word) and data dictionary (Excel): agent capacity, the day-7 re-prioritisation point, out-of-time validation and calibration requirements, dispute/hardship exclusions, business rules and the three fields refreshed at extract (leakage suspects).\par
2. Profile the raw extract with Excel PivotTables - pay rate by product (label variants, BNPL), employment placeholders, account status at extract (leakage), prior delinquencies - and save the pivot workbook (artifact 1).\par
3. Run the preprocessing notebook and fix a date parser that silently misread day-first dates into the wrong cohort months and zero incomes that made the payment-burden ratio infinite; review de-duplication, label mapping and business-rule removals (artifacts 2-3).\par
4. Run the training notebook: show the monthly decline in pay rate, fix a training window that overlapped the calibration month, and remove total_contact_attempts_30d after permutation importance exposed it as post-day-7 leakage; compare logistic regression and gradient boosting on May (artifacts 4-5).\par
5. Run the testing notebook on the untouched June cohort: ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, isotonic calibration fitted on May (predicted pay rate 45.7% raw to 40.0% vs 36.9% actual), SHAP drivers, and fix a prioritisation that ranked by p x amount instead of (1 - p) x amount; compare unpaid amount covered against the highest-days-past-due rule (artifacts 6-7).\par
6. Run the work-list notebook: calibrated scores, specialist routing of dispute/hardship accounts, ranking by expected unpaid amount cut at agent capacity (artifact 8), and package model, calibrator, metrics and README (final output 3).\par
7. Validate the work list in Excel - count vs capacity, exclusions, expected vs actual unpaid, coverage vs today's rule (final output 1) - and complete the summary deck from the template with results and the calibration chart (final output 2).\par
Validation criteria: June (out-of-time) ROC-AUC >= 0.78; beats the logistic baseline on PR-AUC; calibration gap reduced; no field captured after day 7 used; work list equals agent capacity, excludes dispute/hardship accounts and covers more unpaid amount than highest-days-past-due-first.\par
